
  Agent Chassis — what this project is

  This is a distributed, multi-tenant, multi-agent orchestration platform written primarily in Go (module github.com/gqls/agentchassis). It lets you compose "AI agents" that talk to each
  other over Kafka to carry out complex workflows — the flagship use case being automated content/website creation (there's heavy tooling around a "vonc" content-generation pipeline).

  The core idea: the "chassis"

  The central concept is the agent chassis (platform/agentbase/, cmd/agent-chassis/main.go). Every agent runs the same generic binary but is specialised at runtime via environment variables
  (AGENT_TYPE, KAFKA_TOPIC, KAFKA_CONSUMER_GROUP). Each agent is simultaneously:

  - a Server (AgentServer) — consumes work requests from its Kafka topic, and
  - a Client (AgentClient) — consumes responses from other agents it delegated to.

  So every agent is both a worker and an orchestrator. An agent can receive a task, decompose it, fan work out to other agents (reasoning, image generation, etc.), collect their responses,
  and continue its workflow. This eliminates a central orchestrator as a single point of failure (platform/agentbase/README.md, docs/architecture/004-agent-chassis-architecture.md).

  Architecture / how it communicates

  - Kafka is the message backbone. Topic conventions:
    - system.agent.{type}.requests/process — incoming work
    - system.responses.{type} — responses from delegated agents
    - system.errors.{type}, system.notifications.ui, system.commands.workflow.resume
  - Two PostgreSQL databases (pgx driver, pgbouncer in front):
    - Templates DB — configuration: agent_definitions (the "type" of each agent, its default workflow as JSONB).
    - Clients DB — runtime/per-tenant: agent_instances (config overrides per client) plus shared orchestrator_state (tracks each workflow run by correlation_id, status, workflow_plan,
  execution_path, awaited_steps, etc.).
  - Multi-tenancy is first-class: requests carry client_id, correlation_id, and agent_instance_id headers; each client gets its own schema.

  The services (in cmd/ and deployments/kustomize/services/)

  - agent-chassis — the generic agent runtime (specialised per deployment).
  - reasoning-agent, content-creator-agent — concrete "smart" agents (internal/agents/).
  - core-manager — central management service.
  - auth-service — authentication (JWT), backed by its own auth DB.
  - Adapters (internal/adapters/) — bridges to external capabilities: image-generator-adapter, web-search-adapter, web-scrape-adapter, analyser-adapter, git-adapter, thunder-adapter,
  ollama-adapter (local LLM).
  - Infra/support: kafka-scheduler, remote-job-spawner, agent-job-cleanup, database-backup, pgbouncer, wireguard, github-actions-runner, business-intel/vet-intel (domain analytics).

  Frontends (frontends/)

  admin-dashboard, user-portal, and agent-playground — web UIs for operating and experimenting with the system.

  Deployment

  Fully Kubernetes-native via Kustomize (deployments/kustomize/services/*/overlays/production/uk_001/). Each service has a base + production overlay (the uk_001 overlays are what's currently
  modified on your branch). Rich platform/ libraries back it all: kafka, config, discovery, governance, resilience, observability, memory, evolution, storage, aiservice.

  Tech stack summary

  Go · Kafka · PostgreSQL (pgx/pgbouncer) · Kubernetes/Kustomize · Zap logging · JWT auth · Ollama for local LLM inference · web frontends.

  ---
  In one sentence: it's a Kafka-orchestrated, Kubernetes-deployed fleet of interchangeable Go "agents" — each one both worker and orchestrator — that collaborate to run configurable
  multi-agent AI workflows (notably automated content/website generation) for multiple tenant clients.

